# <%= name %>

A Prismatic custom component, scaffolded by `prism components:init`.

| Command                    | What it does                                                        |
| -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `npm test`                 | Runs the unit tests                                                 |
| `npm run build`            | Bundles the component for the Prismatic platform (`dist/`)          |
| `npm run publish:platform` | Builds, then publishes the component to Prismatic                   |
| `npm run build:npm`        | Compiles the component as an npm package (`npm-dist/`), see below   |

For documentation on writing custom components, see
https://prismatic.io/docs/custom-connectors/.

## Using this component from a code-native integration

Add the component to your integration's `package.json`. The integration must
also depend on `@prismatic-io/spectral`.

You do not need to publish the component to Prismatic yourself.
`prism integrations:import` publishes it whenever an integration depends on it
(skipping the publish when nothing has changed). `npm run publish:platform`
publishes it directly.

### From a git repository (no registry needed)

Pin a tag, branch, or commit. npm builds the package on install, so nothing
needs to be committed or published.

GitHub:

```json
{
  "dependencies": {
    "<%= name %>": "github:your-org/<%= name %>#v1.0.0"
  }
}
```

GitLab (gitlab.com):

```json
{
  "dependencies": {
    "<%= name %>": "gitlab:your-group/<%= name %>#v1.0.0"
  }
}
```

### From a tarball vendored into the integration

```sh
npm pack                                  # in this directory, writes <%= name %>-0.0.1.tgz
```

```json
{
  "dependencies": {
    "<%= name %>": "file:./vendor/<%= name %>-0.0.1.tgz"
  }
}
```

### From a local checkout, while developing

```json
{
  "dependencies": {
    "<%= name %>": "file:../<%= directory %>"
  }
}
```

npm does not build linked directories, so run `npm run build:npm` here after
each change. Because the link resolves to this directory's `node_modules`, make
sure both projects use the same `@prismatic-io/spectral` version.

### From a workspace in an npm monorepo

When the integration and this component live in the same repository, list both
as npm workspaces in the repository's root `package.json`:

```json
{
  "private": true,
  "workspaces": ["components/*", "integrations/*"]
}
```

Then depend on the component by name with an ordinary version range; npm links
the workspace instead of fetching it from a registry:

```json
{
  "dependencies": {
    "<%= name %>": "*"
  }
}
```

Run `npm install` from the repository root. npm runs this package's `prepare`
script for the workspace, which builds it, and hoists `@prismatic-io/spectral`
to the root so the integration and the component share one copy, as long as
their spectral ranges agree. After
changing the component, rebuild it with `npm run build:npm --workspace <%= name %>`.
npm does not support the `workspace:*` protocol that pnpm, Yarn and Bun use.

### From an npm registry

Remove `"private": true` from `package.json`, then `npm publish` to your registry
and depend on the version as usual.

### Using the component

```ts
import { configPage, flow } from "@prismatic-io/spectral";
import <%= key %> from "<%= name %>";
<% if (example.connection) { -%>

export const configPages = {
  Configuration: configPage({
    elements: {
      // A connection config var, backed by the published component's connection.
      "My Connection": <%= key %>.connections.<%= example.connection %>({ stableKey: "my-connection" }),
<% if (example.dataSource) { -%>
      // A data source config var, run in-process; its inputs bind like a trigger's.
      "My Choice": <%= key %>.dataSources.<%= example.dataSource %>({
        stableKey: "my-choice",
        values: { <%= example.connectionInput %>: { configVar: "My Connection" } },
      }),
<% } -%>
    },
  }),
};
<% } -%>

export const myFlow = flow({
  name: "My Flow",
  stableKey: "my-flow",
<% if (example.trigger) { -%>
  // A component trigger bound to the flow. A `configVar` binding is read on each invocation;
  // other inputs take their pre-clean values.
  onTrigger: <%= key %>.triggers.<%= example.trigger %>({ <%= example.connectionInput %>: { configVar: "My Connection" } }),
<% } -%>
  onExecution: async (context) => ({
    data: await <%= key %>.actions.<%= example.action %>({
<% if (example.connection) { -%>
      <%= example.connectionInput %>: context.configVars["My Connection"],
<% } -%>
      // ...the action's other inputs, typed from its definition
    }),
  }),
});
```
<% if (example.dataSource) { -%>

A data source can also be called directly, `await <%= key %>.dataSources.<%= example.dataSource %>.perform({ ... })`,
from a `dataSourceConfigVar`'s `perform`.
<% } -%>
